Here's a complete example. I'll create the three files directly:

**Tool class — `WeatherTools.cs`**

```csharp
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;
using System.ComponentModel;
using System.Text.Json;

[McpServerToolType]
public class WeatherTools(HttpClient httpClient, ILogger<WeatherTools> logger)
{
    private const string BaseUrl = "https://api.open-meteo.com/v1";

    [McpServerTool, Description("Get current weather for a location by latitude and longitude.")]
    public async Task<string> GetCurrentWeather(
        [Description("Latitude of the location (e.g. 47.6)")] double latitude,
        [Description("Longitude of the location (e.g. -122.3)")] double longitude,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Fetching weather for ({Lat}, {Lon})", latitude, longitude);

        var url = $"{BaseUrl}/forecast?latitude={latitude}&longitude={longitude}"
                + "&current=temperature_2m,wind_speed_10m";

        var response = await httpClient.GetStringAsync(url, cancellationToken);
        return response;
    }

    [McpServerTool, Description("Search for a city name and return matching coordinates.")]
    public async Task<string> GeolocateCity(
        [Description("City name to search for")] string cityName,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        var url = $"https://geocoding-api.open-meteo.com/v1/search?name={Uri.EscapeDataString(cityName)}&count=3";
        var response = await httpClient.GetStringAsync(url, cancellationToken);
        return response;
    }
}
```

**`Program.cs` — stdio transport**

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;

var builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);

// CRITICAL: stdio uses stdout for JSON-RPC, so all logs must go to stderr
builder.Logging.AddConsole(options =>
    options.LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Trace);

builder.Services.AddHttpClient();   // registers IHttpClientFactory + HttpClient

builder.Services.AddMcpServer()
    .WithStdioServerTransport()
    .WithToolsFromAssembly();        // discovers all [McpServerToolType] classes

await builder.Build().RunAsync();
```

---

### How DI works for MCP tools

The SDK supports **two injection styles**:

**1. Constructor injection (non-static class, shown above)**
The tool class is a regular class with constructor parameters. Services like `HttpClient` and `ILogger<T>` are resolved from the DI container when the class is instantiated. Register them in `Program.cs` (e.g., `AddHttpClient()`). `ILogger<T>` is registered by default.

**2. Method parameter injection (static class)**
```csharp
[McpServerToolType]
public static class Tools
{
    [McpServerTool, Description("Do something")]
    public static async Task<string> DoWork(
        [Description("Input value")] string input,
        HttpClient httpClient,          // ← injected from DI, hidden from schema
        CancellationToken ct = default) // ← also injected
    {
        return await httpClient.GetStringAsync($"/api/{input}", ct);
    }
}
```

**Key rule:** The SDK automatically distinguishes DI services from tool parameters. Types registered in the container (`HttpClient`, `ILogger<T>`, `IMcpServer`, `CancellationToken`, `IProgress<T>`) are **injected at runtime** and **never appear** in the tool's JSON schema that the LLM sees. Only parameters with `[Description]` are exposed to the LLM.

Use **constructor injection** when tools share state or multiple methods use the same services. Use **method injection** for simple stateless tools.
